from pathlib import Path
import json
C=[]
D='https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-neural-networks/why-conv.html'
P='https://d2l.ai/chapter_convolutional-neural-networks/pooling.html'
A='https://d2l.ai/chapter_multilayer-perceptrons/mlp.html'
V='https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11929'
T='https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762'
R='https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385'
F='https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.03144'
W='https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.14030'
def add(title,media='',body=None,take='',notes='',layout='wide',weight=2,refs=None,tag='',table=None,labels=None):
 C.append(dict(title=title,media=media.split(',') if media else [],body=body or [],take=take,notes=notes,layout=layout,weight=weight,refs=refs or [D],tag=tag,table=table,labels=labels,caption=None))
add('Computer vision','car-logo.png',take='How useful representations became easier to learn',notes='Introduce the lecture as a design story. We will learn useful visual patterns, ask why the network design changed, then extend the same reasoning to context and attention. Examples are digits and objects in driving scenes. No in-class coding or software exercise. The sequence is pedagogical, not a claim that inventions followed one linear path.',layout='cover',weight=1)
add('Recognition must survive changes in appearance','car_day.png,car_night.png',['The pixels change with lighting.','The object category can stay the same.'],'The goal is to learn what remains useful across examples.','Ask students what they rely on to identify the car at night. Shape, parts and their arrangement can remain helpful when color and brightness change. Generated teaching scenes, not model predictions. This establishes the purpose before nodes or kernels.',layout='pair',labels=['Daylight','Night'],tag='The recognition problem')
add('A prediction has to match the task','writing.png,detection.png',['Classification assigns an image a category.','Detection also estimates where each object is.'],'The output and training labels must match the question.','Connect digit recognition to object recognition. The five-output example later restricts digits to 0–4 for readability. A five-class object recognizer could use five different category labels. Multiple objects in one scene need an appropriate output, such as boxes and classes; five class scores alone do not locate them. The detection image is a published YOLO still, not a transformer result.',layout='pair',labels=['Which digit?','Which objects, and where?'],refs=[D,'https://pjreddie.com/darknet/yolo/'])
add('A fully connected network learns combinations','dense.png',['Each hidden node combines pixel values using learned weights.','The output nodes turn those responses into class scores.'],'N controls capacity. More nodes also mean more work.','Explain one connection as an adjustable influence. Pixel values supply the inputs; hidden responses depend on weighted combinations and activations. The shown four hidden circles stand for a chosen N, and the input column uses an ellipsis. This is the baseline neural network before convolution. Ask for a trade-off in choosing N, but do not spend a slide on architecture tuning.',layout='split',weight=2.5)
add('Training adjusts the whole network','training.gif',['A wrong prediction creates an error signal.','Repeated small updates change how the network combines its inputs.'],'Learning is guided by the task, not by hand-labeling every feature.','Walk through the four stages: digit3 predicted1, correct label3, small updates, repeat. Loss measures error; backpropagation computes sensitivity of loss to weights; the optimizer updates weights. Introduce those terms briefly after the purpose is understood. Individual updates can help one example and harm another. Contrast training with inference: new images normally use the learned weights without updating them.',layout='split',weight=3)
add('Images make full connectivity expensive','dense_growth.gif',['A bigger image creates more input values.','Each extra input adds connections to the hidden layer.'],'Could a useful visual feature depend on only a small neighborhood?','Watch the input column grow while the hidden layer stays fixed. The drawn nodes are representative, with ellipses for the remaining pixels. Highlighting emphasizes the added connections. A dense classifier is possible, but it does not build in the spatial structure of images. Doubling width and height gives four times the pixels and first-layer connections for fixed N. Ask whether detecting a small edge requires every distant pixel. This motivates an architectural assumption rather than claiming dense networks cannot recognize images.',layout='split',weight=2)
add('Local connections match local visual structure','local_response.gif',['A small neighborhood can contain an edge, a corner or a texture.','A learned filter converts that neighborhood into a response.'],'A feature response describes evidence, not a new object label.','Distinguish the input from the learned representation. Follow the highlighted neighborhood, the learned filter and its response. The input image still contains every pixel. One response depends on a nearby patch, illustrated with nine values. A filter is a weighted combination, not necessarily an average. Local receptive fields helped inspire early neural vision models, including Neocognitron in 1980. Spatial locality is a useful starting assumption, not a complete model of vision.',layout='split',weight=2.5)
add('Useful patterns can appear in many positions','digit_left.png,digit_right.png',['The same strokes occur in both image positions.'],'A detector learned on the left should also be useful on the right.','Pause for students to propose sharing a detector. The static pair shows the same digit at two positions in equal-sized image frames. There is no measured model prediction. Convolution shifts responses with the input; it does not by itself guarantee an unchanged final classification. This motivates reusing weights across positions.',layout='pair',labels=['Near the left side','Near the right side'],weight=2)
add('Convolution applies one filter at many locations','05_convolution.gif',['Move the same learned weights across the image.','Collect the responses into a feature map.'],'Many filter applications can share one set of weights.','Trace the animation. Sliding applies the weights; training changes them. The feature map preserves where a pattern responds. The animation is a schematic operation, not output measured on the running digit. Do not multiply every cell. If asked about padding, explain that values around the border allow edge positions to be processed.',layout='split',weight=2.5)
add('Several filters describe different aspects of an image','filter_bank.gif',['Different filters can respond to different useful patterns.','Several response maps give the next layer more evidence.'],'More filters add capacity, computation and memory.','Follow each filter and its response map in turn, then view all three together. This highlighting is for explanation; filters can be computed in parallel. The maps are illustrative. Learned channels need not correspond to named human features. Multiple filters are different sets of weights; moving one filter through many positions does not create new sets of weights. This is the distinction behind the number-of-kernels question.',layout='split',weight=2)
add('Pooling summarizes nearby responses','pooling_motion.gif',take='Fewer responses reduce later work, while some detail disappears.',notes='Follow the highlighted group as the animation fills one output cell at a time in the fixed 4-by-4 example. In each nonoverlapping 2-by-2 block, max pooling retains 4,5,3,7. It loses other values and exact within-group location. Pooling reduces spatial resolution, not feature-channel count here. It is a design option; strided operations also reduce resolution. Pause briefly to ask what an average would retain differently.',layout='figure',weight=2.5,refs=[P])
add('Detail matters when the object is small','road.png,pedestrian_detail.png',['A nearby car covers many pixels.','A distant pedestrian may occupy only a small region.'],'Reducing resolution can remove evidence the task needs.','Trace the pedestrian crop to the full scene. It is the same generated image region, not a detection or measured downsampling experiment. Ask why a digit classifier may tolerate some spatial reduction while a road detector must preserve small-object evidence. This consequence of pooling will later motivate multiscale features.',layout='pair',labels=['The full scene','A small region can matter'],weight=2)
add('Later layers combine simple evidence into richer patterns','hierarchy_motion.gif',['Edges and curves can combine into parts and their arrangement.'],'A useful representation makes the final category easier to distinguish.','Use the illustration as intuition, not an assertion that real channels literally store labeled wheels or doors. Repeated layers mix earlier responses and gather evidence over larger regions. A wheel alone is ambiguous; its arrangement with windows and body structure is more informative. Return briefly to how strokes combine into a digit.',layout='figure',weight=2.5)
add('The CNN learns a representation for its prediction','cnn_flow.gif',['Local filters find evidence. Later layers combine it.','A task-specific output uses the learned representation.'],'Locality, shared weights and composition form the core idea.','Follow the highlighted stages from the digit to the class-score outputs. This is a forward pass using already learned weights; the animation does not show weight updates. Trace the complete CNN once, with activations omitted for visual simplicity and introduced next. This is a conceptual network, not a universal required order. LeNet demonstrates the handwriting application. AlexNet later showed how learned representations could scale to natural-image recognition with data, GPUs and training improvements.',layout='figure',weight=2,refs=[D,'https://bottou.org/papers/lecun-98h','https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2012/hash/c399862d3b9d6b76c8436e924a68c45b-Abstract.html'])
add('ReLU acts on the filter responses','relu_responses.gif',take='Positive values stay. Negative values become zero.',notes='Use one fixed map of filter responses, not raw input pixels. Read the left map once: rows 2,-1,3 and -2,1,-3. Negative entries are highlighted, then the right map becomes 2,0,3 and 0,1,0. The last stage emphasizes the retained positive responses. The original connections and learned weights are still present. ReLU is a nonlinear response rule, while backpropagation and an optimizer perform learning. Its positive branch remains responsive without saturating.',layout='figure',weight=2,refs=[A])
add('Leaky ReLU keeps negative inputs responsive','leaky_sensitivity.gif',take='A small negative slope keeps this learning path responsive.',notes='First pose the limitation: if a unit stays negative across relevant training examples, ReLU can stay flat and block the local learning signal through that activation. One negative response is not a dead unit. Keep the before-and-after example visible throughout: the input changes from -2 to -1. ReLU stays at 0, while Leaky ReLU with illustrative fixed slope 0.1 changes from -0.2 to -0.1. This shows sensitivity, not a complete training update or an improvement guarantee. A small change can affect the leaky response, leaving a nonzero local derivative. PReLU learns the slope instead; that distinction is optional.',layout='figure',weight=2.5,refs=['https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.01852','https://ai.stanford.edu/~amaas/papers/relu_hybrid_icml2013_final.pdf'])
add('The training examples shape what the model learns','car_day.png,car_night.png,car_rain.png,car_occluded.png',take='A model needs useful evidence across the conditions where it will operate.',notes='Show the same car under lighting, weather and occlusion changes. Invite students to identify what remains and what disappears. These are generated scenarios with no model scores. An architecture cannot reliably learn every relevant variation from examples that never show it. Representative data and task-appropriate augmentation address a different problem from connectivity.',layout='four',labels=['Daylight','Night','Rain','Occlusion'],weight=2.5)
add('Good performance on familiar images is not enough','car_day.png,car_occluded.png',['Training examples change the weights.','Unseen examples test whether the learned patterns carry over.'],'Evaluate the conditions that matter, not only an average score.','Explain train versus evaluation data in words. Separate held-out validation used for design decisions from a final test set used for evaluation. Different writing styles, small objects and night scenes can fail differently. Data augmentation must preserve the label. Dropout can discourage dependence on a few activations during training, but is not permanent pruning. Keep that as optional context rather than another operator lesson.',layout='pair',labels=['Learn from examples','Check on new examples'],weight=2)
add('Deeper networks need a reliable path for learning','residual_motion.gif',['More layers can build richer features but be harder to optimize.','A shortcut preserves the input while a block learns a correction.'],'ResNet made very deep networks easier to train.','Follow the highlighted input, learned correction and bypass, then their addition. These stages separate the two contributions for explanation; they are not four training iterations. Explain existing representation plus correction, without equations. A deeper plain network can have worse training error; extra capacity does not guarantee the optimizer finds a better solution. ResNet is not only a remedy for overfitting or vanishing gradients. Historical anchor: 2015 preprint, CVPR2016.',layout='split',weight=2.5,refs=[R])
add('Different object sizes need evidence at different scales','pyramid_flow.gif',['Fine maps preserve more spatial detail.','Coarser maps contain features built over broader regions.'],'Feature pyramids connect these levels so both can help detection.','Return to the small pedestrian. Follow the animation: the CNN develops several feature scales; the coarse features start the top-down path; each later step combines that context with a finer map. In the FPN mechanism, the top-down path and lateral connections enrich finer maps with more developed features. Do not teach sizes or individual operations. FPN2017 is one answer to the scale problem, distinct from the trainability problem of ResNet. It cannot restore evidence absent from the sensor.',layout='figure',weight=2,refs=[F])
add('Recognition also has a computation budget','road.png',['More layers, filters and pixels can increase the work per image.','A vehicle needs useful predictions within its time and energy budget.'],'The best design depends on the errors and delays the task can tolerate.','Discuss a small improvement in average recognition that costs too much latency. MobileNet2017 is a brief example: it separates spatial filtering from channel mixing to reduce work. Do not show another full architecture or operation count. Runtime depends on the hardware and implementation, so fewer mathematical operations do not always mean proportionally less latency.',layout='split',weight=2,refs=['https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.04861'])
add('CNN evolution addressed different bottlenecks',take='These are complementary ideas, not a mandatory replacement sequence.',notes='Read by problem rather than memorizing architecture names. LeNet represents local sharing for handwriting. AlexNet combined scale, data, GPUs, ReLU and regularization. ResNet addressed deep optimization; FPN addressed scale. This is selected history, not the full chronology. Earlier pooling and activation ideas predate many named architectures.',layout='table',weight=2,refs=['https://bottou.org/papers/lecun-98h','https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2012/hash/c399862d3b9d6b76c8436e924a68c45b-Abstract.html',R,F],table=[['Bottleneck','Design response','Example'],['Repeated local patterns','Local connections and shared weights','LeNet, 1998'],['Large-scale recognition','More data and practical deep training','AlexNet, 2012'],['Difficult deep optimization','Preserve information through shortcuts','ResNet, 2016'],['Objects at different sizes','Combine features across resolutions','FPN, 2017']])
add('Recognizing objects does not explain their relationships','scene.png',['The same categories appear in both scenes.','The pedestrian’s position relative to the road changes.'],'What information should the pedestrian region use from elsewhere?','Pause for answers. Crosswalk and vehicle position can help interpret local evidence. The scene is generated and a still image does not establish future motion or intent. This motivates representations that integrate context; it does not assert that object recognition alone solves driving.',weight=2.5)
add('Convolution gathers context using shared filter weights','dilation_motion.gif',['Depth, larger kernels and dilation broaden the area that can contribute.','Standard convolution reuses learned mixing weights at inference.'],'Attention lets the contributions depend on the current image.','Be accurate: CNNs can capture long-range dependencies. The animation shows a dilated filter sampling spaced input locations; blue is input and cyan is output. The contrast is standard convolution versus input-dependent attention, not local versus intelligent. Large kernels do not inherently erase details; spatial reduction can. There are dynamic convolution variants, outside this introduction. Attention offers a different way to combine distant evidence.',layout='split',weight=2,refs=[D,T])
add('Language offers a useful analogy',take='A useful relationship can connect distant positions.',notes='Read the sentence and ask what it refers to. Animal helps interpret it in this sentence. Attention predates the 2017 Transformer; the Transformer made it central to sequence processing. The point is context-dependent information sharing, not a language-model history or measured attention result.',layout='language',weight=2,refs=[T])
add('A vision transformer begins with patch features','patches_motion.gif',['Represent each patch with a learned vector of feature values.','Add position information so the model can use the image layout.'],'A patch is an image region, not necessarily an entire object.','Trace the image, the four regions, their learned feature vectors and added position information. Four patches are used for readability, not as a fixed model specification. A patch can include a part, several objects or background. Original ViT embeds nonoverlapping patches and adds learned position embeddings. Position matters because identical patch contents rearranged can describe a different scene. Skip embedding matrices and patch-size arithmetic.',layout='split',weight=2.5,refs=[V])
add('Attention updates a patch using context','attention_motion.gif',take='Compare patch features, weight their contributions, then combine them.',notes='Follow comparison, unequal weighting, gathering contributions and the updated pedestrian representation in the loop. The model produces weights from the current token features and forms a weighted combination of value features. The lines are invented for teaching; actual attention need not select the semantic relationships shown. Tokens can also attend to themselves. The output is an updated feature vector, not an object label or a human explanation.',layout='figure',weight=3,refs=[T,V])
add('Training determines which context becomes useful','attention.png',['The model learns how to compare and combine patch features.','The task loss rewards useful final predictions.'],'Attention can also learn misleading associations from the data.','Connect this directly to training in the earlier CNN. We usually do not hand-label desired attention links. Learned projections produce current-image weights. Attention is a mechanism, not guaranteed understanding or a causal explanation. Ask students how an always-present background could become a shortcut. Q/K/V names and matrix formulas remain outside the main lecture.',layout='portrait',weight=2,refs=[T,V])
add('Repeated blocks refine the representation','transformer.png',['Attention shares information between patches.','Small neural networks transform the resulting features.'],'The representation develops over several rounds of processing.','Trace the simplified transformer overview. Real blocks also use residual paths and normalization to support training; mention their purpose without a new derivation. Multiple attention heads can learn different mixing patterns, but each need not represent a named relationship. Original ViT used large-scale training and a classification head. Features can also feed other task outputs.',weight=2.5,refs=[V])
add('The prediction head still has a job to do','writing.png,detection.png',['Image classification produces category scores.','Object detection produces classes and locations.'],'CNN and transformer features can both support these tasks.','Revisit the task distinction now that students know the feature builders. A ViT classifier by itself does not output bounding boxes. DETR is an optional named example of combining CNN features and a transformer for set prediction; do not teach query matching here. The published YOLO still is evidence of the output format, not a DETR result.',layout='pair',labels=['One image-level answer','Several localized answers'],weight=1.5,refs=[V,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.12872','https://pjreddie.com/darknet/yolo/'])
add('Global attention has a cost','patches.png',['Finer patches create more tokens.','Every token comparing with every other token creates many pairs.'],'More detailed input can make attention much more expensive.','At fixed image resolution, smaller patches increase token count. Doubling token count quadruples the number of score pairs in global attention, though full runtime includes other operations. Keep the idea qualitative on the slide. Ask students to suggest fewer comparisons without discarding all context.',layout='split',weight=2,refs=[V])
add('Windowed attention brings locality back','windows_motion.gif',['Compare within local windows to reduce the work.','Shift the windows so information crosses earlier boundaries.'],'Swin combines local attention with a hierarchy of image features.','Watch patches A and B remain in place while the window grouping changes. They start across a boundary and then share a shifted window. Only the central shifted window is emphasized; edge masking and implementation details are omitted. Shifts permit cross-window communication over successive layers, not instant all-image communication in every block. Patch merging creates coarser levels. The causal loop is familiar: a more flexible mechanism introduces cost, so we reuse locality and scale.',layout='split',weight=2,refs=[W])
add('A new architecture does not remove the data problem','car_rain.png,car_occluded.png',['Missing or ambiguous evidence can still cause mistakes.','Context can help, and it can also mislead.'],'Representative evaluation remains necessary for either architecture.','Use two difficult scenarios and ask whether attention guarantees success. No. Occlusion removes evidence; unfamiliar weather changes appearance; background shortcuts can mislead both model families. More data, better sensor evidence and evaluation may be more valuable than changing the architecture.',layout='pair',labels=['Changed appearance','Missing evidence'],weight=1.5,refs=[V])
# Readable quarter-minute pacing with a fixed 75-minute class budget.
total=sum(c['weight'] for c in C);raw=[c['weight']/total*300 for c in C];q=[int(x) for x in raw]
for i in sorted(range(len(C)),key=lambda i:raw[i]-q[i],reverse=True)[:300-sum(q)]:q[i]+=1
t=0
for i,(c,n) in enumerate(zip(C,q)):
 c.update(slide=i+1,minutes=n/4,start=t,end=t+n/4);t+=n/4
 if c['media']==['attention.png']:c['caption']='Illustrative connections, not measured attention'
 if c['media']==['attention_motion.gif']:c['caption']='Illustrative attention weights and features'
 if c['media']==['pooling_motion.gif']:c['caption']='2 × 2 max pooling: keep the largest response in each group'
 if c['media']==['hierarchy_motion.gif']:c['caption']='Illustration of a feature hierarchy'
 if c['slide']==8:c['imageFrames']=True
 if c['media']==['cnn_flow.gif']:c['caption']='Follow the highlighted stages of a forward pass'
 if c['media']==['relu_responses.gif']:c['caption']='The response values change; the connections remain'
 if c['media']==['leaky_sensitivity.gif']:c['caption']='Compare the same small input change through both response rules'
 if c['media']==['pyramid_flow.gif']:c['caption']='Broader context passes down and combines with finer detail'
from process_content import apply_process_revision
apply_process_revision(C)
Path(__file__).with_name('content.json').write_text(json.dumps(C,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False))
print(len(C),'slides;',t,'minutes; tables',[c['slide'] for c in C if c['table']])
